= 2.1 Purpose and Core Principles



== Narrative


The HMTc Program is designed to achieve three simultaneous objectives: protect participating brands from litigation exposure through documented, defensible testing and improvement protocols; protect consumers by incrementally driving down heavy metal exposure in seafood products; and create commercial value through a certification mark that signals verified, ongoing compliance under independent surveillance. These three objectives are not in tension. A program that fails any one of them fails all three, a program that does not reduce brand legal exposure cannot recruit participants; a program that does not drive consumer-exposure reduction has no defensible public-health basis; and a program that does not create commercial value cannot sustain itself across the time horizons required to achieve population-level impact.


The Program's five Governing Principles, published separately and reproduced in the Methodology Appendix of this manual (Part 5.1), are the authority that this Governance specification implements. The principles are: drive contamination reduction through consumer demand; safeguard consumers; safeguard brands that enter the program; ensure legal defensibility; and scale the program to become the de facto standard. No principle is subordinate to any other. Every operational rule in this Part 2 is evaluated against all five; conflicts among principles are surfaced and resolved rather than ignored.



== Operational specification



=== 1.2 Action Levels, Not Safety Thresholds


The HMTc Standards set category-specific concentration limits (action levels) for priority toxic heavy metals in finished seafood products. These action levels are risk-management tools anchored to the best available occurrence data and regulatory benchmarks. They are expressly *not* safety thresholds. Exceeding an action level triggers sourcing, process, and quality control interventions under the Program, but does not by itself indicate that a product is unsafe or that a consumer has been harmed. This distinction is fundamental to the legal and scientific integrity of the Program and shall be reflected in all Program documentation, communications, and mark-usage guidelines.



=== 1.3 ALARA Paradigm


The Program operates under an As Low As Reasonably Achievable (ALARA) paradigm. For metals where no safe exposure threshold is known in children (including lead and inorganic arsenic), the Program requires continuous, feasible reduction of concentrations over time. ALARA is not aspirational language; it is an enforceable obligation within this Program. Brands that achieve certification and then plateau without pursuing further reduction are not in compliance with the spirit or the requirements of this Policy. The ALARA obligation is operationalized through trend monitoring, CAPA requirements, Verification of Effectiveness protocols, and the Standards Ratchet Mechanism described in Part 2.10.



=== 1.4 Concentration-Based Limits


All HMTc action levels are expressed as parts per billion (ppb, equivalent to micrograms per kilogram) on an as-sold basis. The Program does not use per-serving limits. This design prevents circumvention through serving-size manipulation and ensures that compliance is achieved through genuine reduction in contaminant concentrations rather than labeling strategies. Concentration-based limits provide a consistent, testable, and comparable basis for certification across all product categories.



=== 1.5 Legal Compliance Disclaimer


Certification under this Program does not constitute legal advice and does not replace the Licensee's obligation to comply with all applicable laws and regulations in every jurisdiction where the product is sold. If a product's heavy metal content violates a regulatory limit or trigger level in any jurisdiction, that noncompliance is a hard-stop condition under this Policy and will trigger immediate corrective action regardless of the product's certification status. The Program Operator will cooperate with regulatory authorities as required by law.



=== 1.6 International Scope


HMTc applies anywhere seafood products are sold. The Program is operated by Paleo Certified, Inc., a Florida corporation doing business as The Paleo Foundation, with dispute resolution under California law (see Part 5.6). The Program applies operationally to products sold in any jurisdiction.


The 2026 Edition is calibrated against the global open literature aggregated on heavymetalindex.com. A brand selling in any jurisdiction is evaluated against the same per-category HMTc limits derived from the global corpus, with the lowest applicable regulatory cap in the brand's markets of sale serving as a hard upper bound (per the standards-setting principle in Part 5.1 and the regulatory escalation rule in Part 2.11).


*Regional adaptability, roadmap for future Editions.* Future Editions of the Standards will introduce region-specific calibration as the heavymetalindex.com corpus accumulates region-specific evidence at sufficient sample-size totals to support statistically defensible regional pooling. The Governing Principles are universal; their _calibration_ may become regional in a future Edition. When that happens, the operational rule will be: the more stringent of (regional platform limit, global cleanest-achievable benchmark) governs, so that brands selling in higher-contamination regions face standards that incentivize sourcing from cleaner supply chains, the market-demand catalyst Governing Principle 1 names as the program's primary mechanism.


For the 2026 Edition, regional adaptability is named here as a structural commitment to future evolution, not as an operational feature. The honest disclosure is that the current Edition uses global calibration, and that the next Edition's regional-adaptability work will begin once Cat 2 and additional categories ship. A separate EU-domiciled corporate entity for heavymetalindex.com is on the roadmap; that entity will operate the corpus under EU Database Directive 96/9/EC protection once incorporated, complementing the U.S. copyright and trade-secret protection that the 2026 Edition relies on.



=== 1.7 Program-Operator Continuity


The HMTc Program is operated by Paleo Certified, Inc., a Florida corporation doing business as The Paleo Foundation, with operational governance documented in the controlled-document registry. Brand licensees may rely on Program continuity as follows: the license agreement remains operative across changes in Program leadership; the methodology continues to evolve through the Standards Ratchet and the Governing Principles' revision cycle; and if Program operation transfers to another entity (by sale, merger, or governance reorganization), licensees receive written notice with a minimum 12-month grace period before any operational change affects their certification status. The Program Operator publishes annual operational continuity reporting that includes governance composition, financial sustainability, and any pending transition events.



